    "result_text": "The full suite is green \u2014 **36 tests run, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country E.164 national-number length validation in the telephone normalization path.\n\n**Change** \u2014 `OwnerRestControllerV1.toE164(...)` (`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`):\n\n- Added a `NATIONAL_NUMBER_LENGTHS` map declaring the required national-digit count per known country calling code: `\"1\"` \u2192 10 (NANP), `\"61\"` \u2192 9 (Australia).\n- After the existing generic 8\u201315 digit E.164 check, the method now detects the country code (matching the **longest** known prefix first, so a shorter code can't mask a longer one) and, when the code is recognised, rejects the number with an `InvalidOwnerFieldsException` (\u2192 HTTP 400) if its national-number length doesn't match.\n\n**Design notes:**\n- Only *recognised* country codes are enforced, so numbers like `+64 21 123 456` (Cp08's explicit-country-code test) are untouched \u2014 exactly what the existing spec requires.\n- The rule sits on the shared normalization path used by both create and update, and after the general length check, so wrong-length inputs like `+61 123` are rejected with 400 as the new Cp26 test expects.\n- `uniqueTelephone()` in the test base produces a 10-digit `04XXXXXXXX` \u2192 9 national digits under `+61`, which satisfies the new rule, keeping the whole telephone rule chain valid.\n\nAll checkpoints pass, including the new `Cp26Tests.errorRejectsWrongNationalLength`.",
